# Comment Intent Standard

When modifying code, write newcomer-friendly comments so first-time readers can quickly understand intent and flow.

## Required

- Add intent comments before core logic blocks, not only one-liners.
- Explain why this logic exists, what problem it solves, and key assumptions.
- Cover at least these areas when touched:
  - async/concurrency coordination
  - state transitions and scene flow
  - resource loading/release lifecycle
  - fallback and error handling branches
- For modified methods with non-trivial flow, include at least one summary comment that describes the overall step sequence.
- Keep comments concise but specific enough for a newcomer to follow.

## Avoid

- Do not add trivial comments that restate syntax (for example: assign value to variable).
- Do not spam comments on obvious getters/setters or single-line straightforward statements.
- Do not add stale TODO-style comments without action.

## Good examples

```csharp
// Use one-shot init gate to avoid duplicate await registration under chunk load concurrency.
await EnsureTemplateLoaderInitializedAsync(ct);
```

```csharp
// Keep GlobalRoot across scene switches so AssetService is initialized once.
DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
```

```csharp
// Transition order is Exit -> Enter to guarantee old scene resources are released
// before initializing the next state's heavy dependencies.
await stateMachine.ChangeStateAsync(next, ct);
```
